# Direct brief principles

Briefs govern bounded deliveries such as a short-form action, simple demonstration, turntable, pose study, motion test, or visualization whose complete intent fits one delivery/shot/observation hierarchy. They are not a compressed substitute for a film that needs independent narrative refinement.

## Brief information structure {#brief-information-structure}

Arrange the current brief H2, H3, or H4 so a reader can locate its owned delivery promise, shot contribution, or observation predicate; the conditions and inherited basis under which it applies; the result to observe; the boundary that would falsify it; and any outcomes allocated to its descendants without reconstructing one decision from scattered fragments. Use prose, lists, or tables according to the material rather than a fixed template.

Give each paragraph, list, or table one information role or one inseparable role bundle. Split mixed functions, join fragments that cannot be judged coherently, and remove summaries that repeat the addressed descendants instead of organizing them.

Review question: can a reviewer locate this unit's owner, conditions, observation, falsifying boundary, and any descendant results without reconstructing them from unrelated fragments?

This principle owns information arrangement inside one addressed brief unit. The brief unit-addressability obligation owns whether each independently changeable delivery, shot, or observation has its own address, and the observable-progression obligation owns complete allocation across the hierarchy.

Sources: [Purdue OWL on paragraph focus and support](https://owl.purdue.edu/owl/graduate_writing/introduction_to_writing/documents/drafting-your-document/organization-at-the-paragraph-level.pdf); [George Mason University on reverse outlining paragraph functions and order](https://writingcenter.gmu.edu/writing-resources/writing-as-process/reverse-outlining); [NASA systems engineering handbook on verification objectives and traceability](https://www.nasa.gov/reference/systems-engineering-handbook/)

## No narrative smuggling {#no-narrative-smuggling}

Every brief H2, H3, and H4 keeps its local action inside the bounded observation contract and excludes independently authored causal character change, audience revelation, or scene-to-scene dramatic inheritance. Once the result requires any of those as a separate decision layer, the production migrates to the film kind and authors the full ladder.

Review question: would a viewer need to infer why a character chose an action or what a revelation means for the brief to succeed?

Sources: [Plan-and-Write on an explicit planning layer](https://doi.org/10.1609/aaai.v33i01.33017378); [Zacks and colleagues on event segmentation](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8710938/)
